How ArgoCD works under the hood (and why it matters)

To really master ArgoCD, you need to understand how it’s built. Today, we’re lifting the hood on its architecture so you know what’s happening during every Git-to-cluster sync.

⚙️ Core Components of ArgoCD
Application Controller
Watches Application CRDs in your cluster
Continuously compares desired state (from Git) vs live state (in cluster)
Triggers syncs to bring them in alignment
Repository Server
Clones and renders manifests from Git repositories
Supports Helm, Kustomize, Jsonnet, plain YAML
Keeps the rendering process isolated for security and speed
API Server (Dex + RBAC)
Serves the web UI and CLI
Manages authentication and RBAC policies
Integrates with SSO (GitHub, LDAP, OIDC, etc.)
ArgoCD UI / CLI / REST API
Offers full observability: sync status, health, diff
Supports manual overrides and rollbacks
CLI (argocd) is scriptable and team-friendly
🔁 The Sync Loop
Here’s what happens in the background:

You define your app manifests in Git
ArgoCD clones the repo via the Repo Server
Application Controller compares desired vs live state
If there’s a drift (or a change in Git), it triggers a sync
K8s objects are created/updated — no manual kubectl needed
UI/CLI/API show live sync + health status
💡 Bonus: Sync policies (manual, auto, hooks) let you customize exactly how this flow behaves.

💭 Why This Matters
✅ You gain predictable, repeatable deployments
✅ You get self-healing infra based on Git state
✅ You can scale confidently across teams and clusters
✅ You’ll finally be able to treat infra like code — for real
